==========================================================================================================================
  VERDICT
==========================================================================================================================
  cells: 4   beats SPY on total return: 0/4   beats SPY on Sharpe: 3/4
  max-loss cap breached anywhere: NEVER (confirmed across all cells and trades, including 2008/2020)
  catastrophic single-day/-month bar breached anywhere: NO -- the defined-risk structure never breaches the sec-20/26 bar

  filter effect on the CAPPED structure: mean Sharpe filtered +0.61 vs unfiltered +1.09; mean total return filtered +40% vs unfiltered +93%
  Consistent with sec 26: the IV-rank filter is not adding value here either -- UNFILTERED has the
  higher Sharpe, higher DSR, and higher total return in every one of these 4 cells.

  NOTE ON THE 0/4 TOTAL-RETURN RESULT: total return and Sharpe disagree (0/4 vs 3/4) because this study intentionally sizes each new position at a conservative 2% of capital -- the strategy runs at 6% max drawdown vs SPY's 55%, i.e. it is nowhere near fully risking the account SPY B&H risks. Sharpe is size-invariant and is therefore the fairer apples-to-apples comparison; raw total return at matched sizing is not, and is reported for completeness, not as the deciding number.

  PLAIN ANSWER: is the capped credit-spread version a good, consistent, worthwhile return once the
  reduced premium (from buying protection) is accounted for?
  -> MIXED, NOT A CLEAN YES. 3/4 cells beat SPY on Sharpe and the tail risk is
     genuinely bounded (confirmed above, including through 2008/2020) -- capping the loss did its job.
     BUT: 0/4 cells beat SPY on raw total return at the stated conservative sizing, and the
     BEST cell's DSR (reference only) is 0.490, well short of this project's own 0.95
     significance bar for every cell -- so the Sharpe edge shown here is NOT distinguishable from
     noise by this project's usual standard, even though it is nominally positive vs SPY.
     Bottom line: the structure behaves exactly as designed (bounded, no catastrophe) but this
     window does not provide statistically convincing evidence that it is worth trading over simply
     holding SPY -- a plausible small edge, not a demonstrated one.

